  "scope": "Complete unchanged BattlestaffListener (with BattlestaffProduct), GlassCraftingListener, GlassCraftingPulse, GlassMakeListener, GlassMakePulse, GlassProduct, SpikyVambraces, core.api.Container, InputType, Event, Pulse, NodeUsageEvent, IntType, Plugin and Initializable, compiled with Kotlin 1.8.20/JDK 11 against the pinned ConstLib 1.4.28 artifact. ContentAPI's hasLevelDyn, rewardXP, submitIndividualPulse, runTask, sendSkillDialogue and SkillDialogueBuilder and the ResourceProducedEvent declaration are pinned text spliced verbatim. Player, Item, the inventory (a map of amounts: inInventory compares the amount held, a removal succeeds when at least the amount is held, an addition always succeeds), the level, amountInInventory, inInventory, removeItem, addItem, sendMessage, animate (with a number only), playAudio, openInterface, closeInterface, sendInputDialogue (which records the prompt and keeps the handler), Skills.addExperience, the diary manager's finishTask, Entity.dispatch and PulseManager.run (which records the pulse's fields as constructed and keeps it) are recording adapters whose results come from declared case state; they are service inputs, not ports. DiaryType is an adapter enum of the pinned constants' names. The Skills constants and the SkillDialogue constants with forLength are pinned text spliced into adapter shells. UseWithHandler is an adapter with the type constants, the allowed nodes and a recorded addHandler: its run, its lookup by key and its pulses are not covered. SkillDialogueHandler is an adapter that records open() with its type and items and keeps create/getAll overridable: its display, buttons and amount prompts are not covered. InteractionListener and InterfaceListener are adapters that keep what onUseWith and on register. The driver calls SpikyVambraces.handle with an event of two items it names, a use-with lambda through a declared lookup (the pair as used, then flipped) with two Items as its nodes (also for the scenery registration), the interface lambda with a component, opcode and button it names and slot and item 0, the amount prompt's handler with an Integer, and pulse() of the pulse the last run kept, not Pulse.update(): nothing here establishes on which ticks a pulse runs, and a prompt answered with text is not covered. No live world, original Container/Skills arithmetic, interface or option dispatch, dialogue interpreter, diary or item definition behaviour is established by this corpus.",
